Functions and Constants in Namespaces
*************************************

.[perex]
An unqualified call to `strlen()` inside a namespace can represent two entirely different functions in PHP, and looking at a single isolated file, it is impossible to know which one is intended. Because of this, code style tools have to guess on a whole range of common fixes. In most cases, a single line of configuration is all it takes to tell DressCode what exists in your namespaces, and automated fixes immediately stop being risky. Certain names also open the way to [faster function calls |php-compiler-optimizations].


One Name, Two Meanings
======================

Take a look at the following snippet:

```php
namespace App;

if (is_null($user)) {
	// ...
}
```

Which function is actually being called here? Most developers naturally assume it's the global `is_null()` that everyone knows. But PHP resolves this only at runtime: first it checks whether a local `App\is_null()` function exists, and only if it doesn't does it fall back to the global one. All it takes is another file anywhere in your project declaring:

```php
namespace App;

function is_null(mixed $value): bool
{
	return $value === null || $value === '';
}
```

and the call above suddenly does something completely different. The file containing the call doesn't matter in the slightest: the only thing that decides behavior is whether that second file has been loaded.

Constants behave in the exact same way: `PHP_EOL` inside the namespace `App` resolves to `App\PHP_EOL` if defined, and to the global `PHP_EOL` otherwise. Classes do not work this way: an unqualified `Exception` inside `App` always unequivocally means `App\Exception`, and the global class must be written as `\Exception` or imported via `use`.

Why does PHP treat functions differently from classes? When namespaces arrived in PHP 5.3, classes and functions got different answers. For classes, a fallback to the global name combined with autoloading would have been a performance disaster: every `new Exception` would first try to load a file for `App\Exception`. Functions and constants have no autoloading, so they were granted a silent fallback to the global namespace, and code written before namespaces could move into one without a backslash before every `strlen()`. It was a pragmatic compromise for its era; its cost is that the meaning of an unqualified function call is not a property of the file but of the entire running program.


Why Tools Struggle with It
==========================

Code style tools inspect individual files in isolation, not a running process. Faced with `is_null($x)` inside a namespace, a tool encounters the exact same ambiguity you faced above, and it cannot know the answer with certainty. Yet a whole range of useful fixes assume the call is definitely the built-in PHP function:

- rewriting `is_null($x)` to `$x === null`,
- `intval($x)` to a typecast `(int) $x`,
- `sizeof($a)` to `count($a)`,
- `dirname(__FILE__)` to `__DIR__`,
- `get_class()` to `self::class`,
- adding `use function count;` so the compiler can [optimize the call |php-compiler-optimizations].

If the function happened to be your own `App\is_null()`, every one of those edits would silently alter program behavior. A responsible tool must therefore classify such transformations as risky fixes and refuse to make them without explicit permission. PHP CS Fixer flags rules like `is_null`, `no_alias_functions`, and `dir_constant` as risky for this exact reason: they are unsafe if the function is overridden locally.

And this is far from a theoretical concern. A well-known testing pattern relies entirely on this mechanism: libraries like php-mock declare `App\time()` or `App\file_get_contents()` inside a test suite so that code under test in the `App` namespace invokes a mock rather than the real runtime function.


Let's Be Pragmatic
==================

How many custom functions and constants do you actually declare in your namespaces? In almost all codebases, the answer is very few, and often none at all. Functions in PHP **cannot be autoloaded**: for a function to be callable, its file must be explicitly loaded ahead of time via `require` or a `files` entry in `composer.json`. That is why application logic moved permanently into classes long ago, leaving namespaced functions a rare edge case. Function autoloading has been proposed for PHP multiple times, but has never become part of the language.

This is also an excellent reason never to rely on unqualified calls for namespaced functions: if the file containing the function happens not to be loaded, PHP raises no error: it silently calls the global function of the same name, if one exists. An explicit import `use function App\is_null;` won't autoload the file either, but it guarantees that the call fails immediately and clearly with `Call to undefined function App\is_null()`.

The reality is clear: the theoretical ambiguity affects everyone, but real custom functions in namespaces are exceedingly rare. All you need to do is enumerate them.


Tell DressCode What Is What .[#name-resolution]
===============================================

In the most common case, you declare no custom functions or constants in namespaces at all. When that is the case, a single configuration line is all you need:

```neon
nameResolution: certain
```

This single setting tells DressCode that every unqualified function or constant name that a file neither imports nor declares locally is guaranteed to be global. Not "probably," but with absolute certainty.

If you do declare some custom functions or constants in namespaces, list them under the `namespaces` key: functions under `functions` and constants under `constants`. Syntax matches standard `use` statements, including groups in braces. Wrap grouped entries in quotes so NEON doesn't parse them as maps:

```neon
nameResolution: certain

namespaces:
	functions:
		- 'App\Utils\{format, parse}'
		- App\Legacy\helper
	constants:
		- 'App\{VERSION, DEBUG}'
```

DressCode then treats any function in that list as belonging to its namespace wherever it is invoked without an import. For functions, letter case does not matter at all; for constants, case matters only in the constant's own name (not in the namespace path), matching PHP's native behavior.

The lists and `nameResolution` represent two separate concepts, which is why each has its own key. The list is a statement of fact: this function exists. `nameResolution` is a decision: nothing else exists there.

| `nameResolution` | what it means |
|---|---|
| `certain` | The lists are complete; anything not listed is guaranteed to be global. |
| `uncertain` | The lists may be incomplete; identical to omitting the key. |

Under `uncertain`, DressCode acts with extreme caution: it assumes an unqualified name is global, but treats any automated fix relying on that assumption as [risky |configuration#risky-fixes]. It reports the fix as a warning with an explanation on the line below the violation (e.g. <code>''the namespace may declare `is_null()`''</code>), making the fix only when you list the decision in `fixRisky` or pass `--fix-risky`. That is why `uncertain` is the default: placing a decision in your configuration does not imply blanket consent to alter code behavior. The moment you configure `certain`, however, these fixes stop being risky.


Every Rule Benefits
===================

What matters most is *where* this knowledge is applied. DressCode doesn't pass this setting to individual rules in isolation; it provides it to the shared `NameResolver` analysis, which resolves names across the entire tool. Built-in rules query it, and so can [your custom rules |rule-contract#Analyses]. The domain knowledge lives in one central place, so no rule has to maintain its own list of exceptions.

This works just as reliably in reverse, when a rule writes a function name into code itself. The decision `hygiene.aliasFunctions` replaces `sizeof()` with `count()` and writes the new name matching the original formatting: `\sizeof()` becomes `\count()`, and bare `sizeof()` becomes bare `count()`. However, if the file imports or declares its own `count`, or if the configuration declares `count()` in that namespace, it emits `\count()`, ensuring the rewritten code calls the exact same function as before.

All rules read every name with identical consistency, regardless of which rule queries it.


A New Function Does Not Slip Through
====================================

A static list would be a liability if it went stale. That is why DressCode guards the list itself: the moment your configuration specifies `nameResolution: certain`, an internal validation check activates under `namespaces.functions` and `namespaces.constants`. If DressCode encounters a newly declared function in a namespace that is missing from the list, it flags it immediately (doing the same for `const` and `define('App\X', ...)` calls). It even accounts for declarations inside conditions, such as a polyfill wrapped in `if (!function_exists(...))`. The moment anyone adds a function like `App\Utils\slugify()`, running `dresscode check` prompts them to update the configuration:

/--pre .[terminal]
src/Utils/helpers.php
  error  12:10  Function `App\Utils\slugify()` must be listed in `namespaces.functions`.  namespaces.functions
\--

This check is enough because, unlike calls, declarations can be identified statically: they exist directly in the files DressCode inspects. Just be mindful of excluded paths. If you declare a function in a test directory that DressCode skips, and the code calls it without an import, add it to the list manually. Under `uncertain`, this check stays silent because there is nothing to enforce. Under `certain`, not even `--only` can turn it off: fixes that rely on complete lists cannot afford to lose the check that keeps them complete.

You don't need to assemble your initial list by hand either. Running [`dresscode init` |generate-configuration] scans all files in your project paths, discovers all functions and constants declared in namespaces, and writes their lists alongside `nameResolution: certain`. If any file fails to parse (making its declarations unknown), `init` avoids writing `certain` and names the unverified file in a comment. It writes the names of one namespace as a group, one namespace per line (`'Acme\Shop\{formatPrice, parsePrice}'`), and a new name goes into any of the groups, or on its own by its full name.


Helper Functions in Tests
=========================

Test suites frequently declare helper functions directly inside their test namespace, such as `App\Tests\createUser()`. Adding these to your global configuration and maintaining them would be tedious noise. For test directories, simply add a scoped [override |configuration#Different Values for Part of the Project] stating that lists are incomplete there:

```neon
nameResolution: certain

overrides:
	- paths: [tests]
	  nameResolution: uncertain
```

DressCode will then inspect files in `tests` cautiously: fixes requiring global functions will only be reported as risky warnings, and the check for unlisted declarations remains silent. Production files in `src` continue to benefit fully from the certainty. This separation works because production code never calls test helpers, meaning those functions have zero influence on name resolution in `src`.

One exception deserves careful attention: the mocked function pattern discussed [above |#Why Tools Struggle with It]. If a test declares `App\time()` to force production code in the `App` namespace to invoke a mock, that change in behavior targets calls in `src`, not in the tests. Such a function definitely belongs in the project's root `namespaces.functions` list so that rules in `src` leave it alone.


Functions Declared by a Framework
=================================

Sometimes you write application code directly inside a framework's namespace because the framework's architecture was designed for it. Symfony, for instance, directs service configurations into files beginning with `namespace Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator;`, specifically so that developers can invoke helper functions like `service()`, `param()`, or `env()` without import statements. Those functions are declared by Symfony itself inside `vendor/`, where DressCode does not look.

DressCode understands these framework helpers natively: whenever a project installs `symfony/dependency-injection`, it automatically adds those functions to the internal `namespaces.functions` list without requiring a single line of configuration, regardless of which coding standard you use. Running `dresscode config` lists them under the `Names` line, attributed to `DressCode for symfony/dependency-injection`.

Other packages can supply their own namespace lists through the `namespaces` key in their [data files |replacement-maps], merging seamlessly with your configuration. However, neither a package nor a preset can set the `nameResolution` key: they understand the framework, but they cannot speak for your own application code.


How Global Names Are Written
============================

How global functions and constants are written in code is controlled by the [`qualification` |decisions/qualification] section. Every name can be represented in three ways, and each decision key accepts one of three values: `bare` leaves the name bare relying on runtime fallback, `imported` requires an explicit `use function` or `use const` import, and `backslashed` mandates a leading backslash.

```neon
qualification:
	function:
		global: bare
		optimized: imported
	constant:
		global: bare
		optimized: imported
```

Under this configuration, ordinary global functions and constants remain clean and bare, while those the compiler can optimize receive an import:

```php
namespace App;

use function count;
use function in_array;
```

At first glance, this might look redundant: `count()` works perfectly without an import statement. But the sole motivation is performance: with an import, the compiler has compile-time certainty that the function is global and applies its direct opcode shortcut (as detailed in [Faster Function Calls in PHP |php-compiler-optimizations]).

.[note]
Using `optimized: imported` can lengthen your file header with multiple `use function` lines. You can keep headers compact using [`imports.grouping.function: combined` |decisions/imports#imports.grouping.function], which bundles functions into a single statement: `use function count, in_array, strlen;`. Fewer lines mean fewer tokens: AI agents inspecting the top of a file reach meaningful business logic faster without having to parse through walls of imports. The `nette` standard formats imports this way.

Keys in the section proceed from general to specific:

| key | which names it decides |
|---|---|
| `function.global`, `constant.global` | Standard global functions and constants in namespaces (excluding those governed by `optimized` keys). |
| `function.optimized` | Functions handled specially by the compiler, applied where a call with the given arguments is genuinely optimized. |
| `constant.optimized` | Constants the compiler evaluates downstream: inside conditions or constant expressions. |
| `function.currentNamespace`, `constant.currentNamespace` | Functions and constants belonging to the file's own namespace. |

For some functions, `function.optimized` depends on the arguments: PHP always optimizes `strlen()`, but `dirname()`, `defined()`, and `function_exists()` are optimized only with constant arguments; `sprintf()` is optimized only with a constant format string that contains nothing but `%s` and `%d` specifiers, one for each passed value; and `in_array()` is optimized only with inline array literals. A function falls under this key as soon as a namespace calls it in an optimizable way at least once, after which every reference to that function in the namespace is formatted consistently. A function never invoked in an optimizable way falls back to `function.global`, preventing unhelpful imports from cluttering the file. The `constant.optimized` decision is similarly context-aware, looking at how the constant is used rather than its name. Setting `bare` on these keys indicates that the project prioritizes clean, unadorned code over compiler shortcuts. Each name is governed by exactly one key: if `function.optimized` is set to `keep`, optimized functions remain untouched, and `function.global: bare` strips leading backslashes from `\array_map()` while leaving `\strlen()` and `\count()` intact.

DressCode inserts imports matching the style your file already uses: integrating into existing multi-name statements, into grouped imports for that namespace, and in correct alphabetical order so that decisions in the [`imports` |decisions/imports] section have nothing to complain about. If the file already imports the function under an alias, it reuses that alias. Where an import cannot be added (for instance, due to a naming collision), it falls back to a leading backslash. Conversely, it strips backslashes only where a bare name safely reaches the global function, and automatically removes orphaned imports that are no longer referenced.

This brings us full circle to namespaces. An import statement `use function strlen;` permanently binds calls to the global function. If your project had its own `App\strlen()`, adding that import would break runtime behavior. Under `uncertain`, DressCode therefore treats any transition between bare names and imports or backslashes as risky. Under `certain`, it knows `App\strlen()` does not exist and applies the import safely. Naturally, it never imports a function listed in `namespaces.functions`.

Classes do not suffer from this ambiguity: an unqualified class name in a namespace never falls back to the global namespace at runtime. The keys `class.otherNamespace` and `class.global` therefore only choose between an import and a backslash, and none of their automated fixes are risky. Identifiers without a leading backslash (such as `Model\User` when `use App\Model;` is present) are left alone, as they resolve relative to imports or the file's namespace. In a file without a namespace, leading backslashes are always redundant, and `qualification.leadingBackslash` cleanly removes them.


Summary
=======

- In PHP, an unqualified function or constant inside a namespace resolves either locally or globally, and an isolated file cannot tell which.
- Set `nameResolution: certain` in your configuration (and list custom names under `namespaces` if you have any). All rules will behave with complete certainty, and fixes relying on global names stop being risky.
- Parts of the project where the lists are not complete, such as tests with helper functions, can use an override with `nameResolution: uncertain`.
- Without `certain`, DressCode acts conservatively, applying such fixes only with explicit permission in `fixRisky` or via `--fix-risky`.
- Newly declared functions in namespaces are automatically tracked and validated under `namespaces.functions`; `dresscode init` generates your initial lists.
- Always call namespaced functions using an explicit `use function` import. If the file is not loaded, you will find out immediately via a clear fatal error.
- Certain names let PHP make function calls much faster; see [Faster Function Calls in PHP |php-compiler-optimizations].

{{maintitle: PHP Functions in Namespaces: the Fallback to Global Functions}}
{{description: An unqualified function call in a PHP namespace can mean two different functions. One line of configuration settles it for DressCode, and its fixes stop being risky.}}
